# Shell scripts must keep LF endings.
#
# tools/sync-from-tos.sh runs under bash. On a fresh checkout Git's autocrlf
# turns its line endings into CRLF, and bash then reads the first line as
# `set -euo pipefail\r` and dies with "set: pipefail: invalid option name".
# A long-lived working copy usually still has LF, so this only breaks for
# someone cloning fresh , which is everyone but the author. TOS hit exactly
# this and fixed it the same way.
*.sh text eol=lf

# .hd scripts get copied into a FAT image and read by holyd's lexer. The
# lexer treats \r as whitespace so CRLF parses, but pinning them keeps the
# bytes on the image identical to the bytes in the repository.
*.hd text eol=lf

# Sources, so a diff between this repository and a TOS checkout is about
# content rather than line endings.
*.c text eol=lf
*.h text eol=lf
Makefile text eol=lf

# Binary payloads: never touch, never diff as text.
*.png binary
*.exe binary
*.elf binary
